{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1327 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "63624a88ac31300c3bf4c6396ddec69ab1f0554d04717316a7ce137af1e5183b" :edb/db-before-t 1326 :edb/db-after-t 1327 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534639 50 #inst "2026-10-08T03:03:37.959Z" 13194139534639 true] [17592186110432 1145 "Bringing all the terms to the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & <1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-1 & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $y = x^2-1$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $x= \\pm 1.$\n\nSolving the inequality $x^2-1 < 0$ is equivalent to finding the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=x^2-1$ is below the $x$-axis (where ${\\color{red}y < 0}$).\n\nSince the leading coefficient of the quadratic expression is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, the solution set lies between the roots (but it does not include them), as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/52328002ef1d61eeedb0799bea1eb407d04dda0f064967401cd6f33423cc553c.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\in {\\color{blue}(-1, 1)}.$" 13194139534639 true] [17592186110432 1145 "Bringing all the terms to the left-hand side, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} & < 1 \\\\ x^{2} - 1 & < 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $y = x^2-1$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 1 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 1^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)(x + 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $x= \\pm 1$.\n\nSolving the inequality $x^2-1 < 0$ is equivalent to finding the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=x^2-1$ is below the $x$-axis (where ${\\color{red}y < 0}$).\n\nSince the leading coefficient of the quadratic expression is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, the solution set lies between the roots (but it does not include them), as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1495/Source/Images/c6466800210c6322a6a2ffd68100400b.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\in {\\color{blue}(-1, 1)}$." 13194139534639 false] [17592186110433 1100 "Solve the inequality $x^2< -9$." 13194139534639 false] [17592186110433 1100 "Solve the inequality $x^2< -9.$" 13194139534639 true] [17592186110433 1145 "We can solve this equation using either of the two methods that we've learned.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Inequality*\n\nWe know that the square of *any* real number is greater than or equal to zero. Therefore, it is impossible for the square of a real number to be smaller than $-9$.\n\nSo, the inequality $x^2 < -9$ has no solutions.\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nLet's bring all the terms to the left-hand side,\n$x^2+9 < 0$,\nand then plot the parabola\n\n$$\ny=x^2+9\n$$\n\nThe desired inequality ($y<0$) is never satisfied, so there are no solutions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1495/Source/Images/d1adac9838271ebb5335c01219ac7c41.png)" 13194139534639 false] [17592186110433 1145 "We can solve this equation using either of the two methods that we've learned.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Inequality*\n\nWe know that the square of *any* real number is greater than or equal to zero. Therefore, it is impossible for the square of a real number to be smaller than $-9.$\n\nSo, the inequality $x^2 < -9$ has no solutions.\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nLet's bring all the terms to the left-hand side,\n\n$$\nx^2+9 < 0,\n$$\n\nand then plot the parabola $y=x^2+9.$ The desired inequality ($y<0$) is never satisfied, so there are no solutions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/9f5f8968a32bbc7ae94386aa5fa2237942ebd25ed74a3390bfa440e3c4550002.png)" 13194139534639 true] [17592186110434 1100 "Solve $3x^2-6 > 3+2x^2$." 13194139534639 false] [17592186110434 1100 "Solve $3x^2-6 > 3+2x^2.$" 13194139534639 true] [17592186110434 1145 "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6 & >3+2{x}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2} & >6+3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & >9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation*\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & >\\sqrt{9} \\\\ |x| & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have $x>3$ or $x<-3.$\n\nSo, the solution is $x \\in (-\\infty,-3)\\cup(3,+\\infty).$\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nStarting from $x^2>9$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\nx^2-9 > 0 .\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $y=x^2-9$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $x=\\pm 3.$\n\nSince the leading coefficient of $x^2-9$ is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, the solution to the given inequality ($y>0$) lies outside of the roots (but does not include them).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/1c0d933b969094b2665e539479d5f9f715be881f9e355002c9398e0f23ba9938.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\in (-\\infty,-3)\\cup(3,+\\infty).$" 13194139534639 true] [17592186110434 1145 "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3x^{2} - 6 & > 3 + 2x^{2} \\\\ 3x^{2} - 2x^{2} & > 6 + 3 \\\\ x^{2} & > 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation*\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{2}} \\mid > \\sqrt{9}; \\mid x \\mid \\mid > 3\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have $x>3$ or $x<-3$.\n\nSo, the solution is $x \\in (-\\infty,-3)\\cup(3,+\\infty)$.\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nStarting from $x^2>9$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\nx^2-9 > 0\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $y=x^2-9$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 9 &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 3)(x - 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $x=\\pm 3$.\n\nSince the leading coefficient of $x^2-9$ is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, the solution to the given inequality ($y>0$) lies outside of the roots (but does not include them).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1495/Source/Images/ab45329cb4ffe9bd9528e09a8398de8f.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\in (-\\infty,-3)\\cup(3,+\\infty)$." 13194139534639 false] [17592186110435 1145 "Taking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & \\le 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & \\le \\sqrt{2} \\\\ |x| & \\le \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt 2\\leq x \\leq \\sqrt 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\in [-\\sqrt 2,\\sqrt 2].$" 13194139534639 true] [17592186110435 1145 "Taking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\nx^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\le 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x^{2}}\\end{vmatrix}\\le \\sqrt{2}; \\mid x \\mid \\mid \\le \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt 2\\leq x \\leq \\sqrt 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\in [-\\sqrt 2,\\sqrt 2]$." 13194139534639 false] [17592186110641 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300349 13194139534639 true] [17592186110641 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300356 13194139534639 true] [17592186110641 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300398 13194139534639 true] [17592186110641 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300461 13194139534639 true] [17592186110641 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300468 13194139534639 true] [17592186110642 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300363 13194139534639 true] [17592186110642 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300408 13194139534639 true] [17592186110642 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300419 13194139534639 true] [17592186110642 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300475 13194139534639 true] [17592186110642 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300489 13194139534639 true] [17592186110643 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300370 13194139534639 true] [17592186110643 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300377 13194139534639 true] [17592186110643 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300428 13194139534639 true] [17592186110643 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300437 13194139534639 true] [17592186110643 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300454 13194139534639 true] [17592186110644 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300384 13194139534639 true] [17592186110644 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300391 13194139534639 true] [17592186110644 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300413 13194139534639 true] [17592186110644 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300447 13194139534639 true] [17592186110644 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300482 13194139534639 true] [17592186300349 1017 #edb/ref 17592186300350 13194139534639 true] [17592186300349 1089 "q-115063" 13194139534639 true] [17592186300349 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534639 true] [17592186300349 1100 "Solve the inequality  ${x}^{2}\\ge 49$ using a graphical method." 13194139534639 true] [17592186300349 1113 #uuid "c9f27888-d6d0-5a5b-b428-4bf6ff1558c7" 13194139534639 true] [17592186300349 1145 "Bringing all the terms to the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & \\ge 49 \\\\ {x}^{2}-49 & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $\\,y={x}^{2}-49\\,$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-49 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{7}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-7)(x+7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $x=\\pm 7.$ \n\nSolving the inequality ${x}^{2}-49\\ge 0$ is equivalent to finding the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y={x}^{2}-49$ is above the $x$-axis or intersects the $x$-axis (where $y\\ge 0$).\n\nSince the leading coefficient of the quadratic expression is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, the solution set lies outside the roots (including them), as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/5993bb434fca17beb9842d7963c5f560b4d8ef0bacadea4c120160d2ab93d131.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup [7,\\infty ).$" 13194139534639 true] [17592186300350 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300351 13194139534639 true] [17592186300350 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300352 13194139534639 true] [17592186300350 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300353 13194139534639 true] [17592186300350 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300354 13194139534639 true] [17592186300350 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300355 13194139534639 true] [17592186300350 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534639 true] [17592186300350 1041 "selection" 13194139534639 true] [17592186300350 1104 #edb/ref 17592186300355 13194139534639 true] [17592186300350 1110 #uuid "54587f8f-5d9e-5958-9121-c73acf1bade7" 13194139534639 true] [17592186300351 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" 13194139534639 true] [17592186300351 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534639 true] [17592186300351 1111 #uuid "18839743-9592-5913-9689-7cff96ef106d" 13194139534639 true] [17592186300352 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,-49]\\cup [49,\\infty )" 13194139534639 true] [17592186300352 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534639 true] [17592186300352 1111 #uuid "f16217b5-605e-5406-af34-f15abb295e96" 13194139534639 true] [17592186300353 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]" 13194139534639 true] [17592186300353 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534639 true] [17592186300353 1111 #uuid "91dab6b3-f20f-5efc-a0d8-243664cdff03" 13194139534639 true] [17592186300354 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cap [3,\\infty )" 13194139534639 true] [17592186300354 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534639 true] [17592186300354 1111 #uuid "d2ab027c-dfbe-5f87-b278-8ce7fc05f2c8" 13194139534639 true] [17592186300355 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup [7,\\infty )" 13194139534639 true] [17592186300355 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534639 true] [17592186300355 1111 #uuid "b5b8fba3-8974-5044-96f0-f8cf971c5295" 13194139534639 true] [175921863003